        "limitations": ["Recognition cannot certify production of the square-factor decompositions or their endpoint justification."],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": ["a", "b"],
        "rationales": [
          {"option_id": "a", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "16x^3=16x^2*x; with x>0, sqrt(16x^2)=4x.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "b", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "sqrt0.01=0.1 and sqrt(b^4)=b^2; sqrt25=5 and sqrt(a^6)=a^3 for a>0. The remaining radicands are b and 2a.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "c", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "The proposed right side squares to 16x^7, not 16x^3; x=2 is a counterexample.", "misconception": "Move the monomial x^3 outside unchanged and also leave x under the radical, instead of halving the exponent of the extracted x^2 factor."},
          {"option_id": "d", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "The numeric square factors must become 0.1 and 5, not 0.01 and 25; b=a=1 already disproves both identities.", "misconception": "Extract the variable square powers correctly but move the numeric square factors 0.01 and 25 outside without taking their roots."}
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "n40-equality-sets",
      "source_numbers": ["827"],
      "view": {
        "mode": "single",
        "stem": "For $x\\in\\{2,3,-5,-10\\}$, find every $x$ satisfying $x\\sqrt3=\\sqrt{3x^2}$. For $c\\in\\{-3,1,4,10,-10\\}$, find every $c$ satisfying $c\\sqrt5=-\\sqrt{5c^2}$. Which pair gives the two complete sets, in that order?",
        "figure_ids": [],
        "options": [
          {"id": "a", "text": "$\\{2,3,-5,-10\\}$ and $\\{-3,1,4,10,-10\\}$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "b", "text": "$\\{-5,-10\\}$ and $\\{1,4,10\\}$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "c", "text": "$\\{2,3\\}$ and $\\{-3,-10\\}$.", "figure_ids": []},
          {"id": "d", "text": "$\\{2,3\\}$ and $\\{1,4,10\\}$.", "figure_ids": []}
        ]
      },
      "intent": {
        "task": "Identify the full equality sets from the two opposite sign branches.",
        "source_solution": "sqrt(3x^2)=|x|sqrt3 requires x>=0 in the first equality, giving {2,3}. The second requires c=-|c|, hence c<=0, giving {-3,-10}. Neither original list contains zero.",
        "unit_reason": "The complete sets together reveal the sign distinction. There is no independent click or answer slot for each listed number.",
        "concept_blocks": [7],
        "limitations": ["Selecting whole sets does not prove independently deriving the sign condition or testing every original candidate."],
        "figure_observations": []
      },
      "key": {
        "correct": ["c"],
        "rationales": [
          {"option_id": "a", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "For x=-5 the first left side is negative while its right side is positive; for c=1 the second left side is positive while its right side is negative.", "misconception": "Square both equations, observe identities 3x^2=3x^2 and 5c^2=5c^2, then assume squaring is reversible without checking original signs."},
          {"option_id": "b", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "The first root requires a nonnegative left side and the second negative-root expression a nonpositive left side, opposite to these sets.", "misconception": "Apply the sign conditions to the opposite side: demand x<0 and c>0 rather than x>=0 and c<=0."},
          {"option_id": "c", "truth": "correct", "derivation": "Intersect x>=0 with its original set and c<=0 with its original set to obtain exactly {2,3} and {-3,-10}.", "misconception": null},
          {"option_id": "d", "truth": "incorrect", "derivation": "The first set is correct; positive c cannot equal the strictly negative right side of the second equation.", "misconception": "Keep the first sign condition but discard the leading minus on the second square-root expression, incorrectly demanding c>=0."}
        ]
      }
    },
